  "content": [
    {
      "id": "introduction",
      "title": "Introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Every beginner stares at a piece of code and wonders:&nbsp;<em>is this fast or slow?</em>&nbsp;Time complexity is the tool that answers that. Once you understand it, you'll never look at code the same way.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"https://MyDSA-assets-images.s3.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/dsa-content-images/1781423520790-927033912.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "1-what-even-is-time-complexity",
      "title": "1. What Even Is Time Complexity?",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Time complexity is&nbsp;<strong>not</strong>&nbsp;how many seconds your code takes to run. It's a measure of&nbsp;<strong>how the number of operations grows as the input grows</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Think of it like this:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "You have a list of 10 names to search through → fast",
            "You have a list of 10 million names → how much slower is it?"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Time complexity answers that question in a way that's independent of your computer, your language, or the year.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "We express it using&nbsp;<strong>Big O notation</strong>&nbsp;written as&nbsp;<code>O(something)</code>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "2-the-big-o-cheat-sheet-before-we-dive-in",
      "title": "2. The Big O Cheat Sheet (Before We Dive In)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "items": [],
          "url": "",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "{\"headers\":[\"Notation\",\"Name\",\"Example\"],\"rows\":[[\"O(1)\",\"Constant\",\"Access array by index\"],[\"O(log n)\",\"Logarithmic\",\"Binary search\"],[\"O(n)\",\"Linear\",\"Loop through array once\"],[\"O(n log n)\",\"Linearithmic\",\"Merge sort, Heap sort\"],[\"O(n²)\",\"Quadratic\",\"Nested loops\"],[\"O(2ⁿ)\",\"Exponential\",\"Recursive Fibonacci\"],[\"O(n!)\",\"Factorial\",\"Brute-force permutations\"]]}",
          "text": ""
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">From top to bottom:&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">faster → slower</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">.</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "3-the-3-golden-rules-of-counting",
      "title": "3. The 3 Golden Rules of Counting",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Before we analyze any code, burn these into your brain:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "<strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Rule 1: Drop the constants.</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">O(2n)</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;→&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">O(n)</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">. We don't care if you loop twice; we care about the&nbsp;</span><em style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">shape</em><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;of growth.</span><br>",
            "<strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Rule 2: Drop lower-order terms.</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">O(n² + n)</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;→&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">O(n²)</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">. When&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">n = 1,000,000</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">, the&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">n</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;part is irrelevant noise.</span><br>",
            "<strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Rule 3: Worst case is the default.</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;Unless told otherwise, always analyze the worst possible input.</span><br>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "How to Actually Read Code and Find Complexity",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This is the practical part. Follow these steps every time.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading4",
          "text": "Step 1: A single statement or assignment",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "cpp": "int x = 5;\ncout << x << endl;\nint a = b + c;"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Each of these is&nbsp;<code>O(1)</code>.</strong>&nbsp;No matter how large your data is, these run once. They don't care about&nbsp;<code>n</code>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "Rule of thumb: No loop, no recursion =&nbsp;<code>O(1)</code>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "<hr style=\"border-width: 1px medium medium; border-style: solid none none; border-color: rgb(224, 224, 224) currentcolor currentcolor; border-image: none; margin: 24px 0px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Step 2: A single loop",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "cpp": "for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n    cout << i << endl;\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The loop runs&nbsp;<code>n</code>&nbsp;times. One operation per iteration. That's&nbsp;<strong><code>O(n)</code></strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "What about this?",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "cpp": "for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n    int x = arr[i] * 2;    // O(1)\n    cout << x << endl;     // O(1)\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Still&nbsp;<code>O(n)</code>. The constant number of operations inside the loop doesn't change the shape just multiply&nbsp;<code>n × constant</code>&nbsp;=&nbsp;<code>O(n)</code>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Step 3: Two separate loops (sequential, not nested)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "cpp": "for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n    cout << i << endl;\n}\n\nfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n    cout << j << endl;\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This is&nbsp;<code>O(n) + O(n)</code>&nbsp;=&nbsp;<code>O(2n)</code>&nbsp;→&nbsp;<strong><code>O(n)</code></strong>&nbsp;after dropping the constant.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The key word is&nbsp;<strong>sequential</strong>&nbsp;one after the other. Think of it as two separate trips across your data.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Step 4: Nested loops",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This is where most beginners get tripped up.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "cpp": "for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n    for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n        cout << i << \" \" << j << endl;\n    }\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The outer loop runs&nbsp;<code>n</code>&nbsp;times. For&nbsp;<strong>each</strong>&nbsp;of those, the inner loop runs&nbsp;<code>n</code>&nbsp;times. Total operations:&nbsp;<code>n × n</code>&nbsp;=&nbsp;<strong><code>O(n²)</code></strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Visualize it as a grid:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "cpp": "i=0 → j runs 0,1,2...n\ni=1 → j runs 0,1,2...n\ni=2 → j runs 0,1,2...n\n...\ni=n → j runs 0,1,2...n"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "That's an&nbsp;<code>n × n</code>&nbsp;grid of work =&nbsp;<code>O(n²)</code>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "What if the inner loop depends on the outer?",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "cpp": "for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n    for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {   // j only goes up to i, not n\n        cout << i << \" \" << j << endl;\n    }\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Operations:&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">0 + 1 + 2 + ... + (n-1)</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;=&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">n(n-1)/2</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;→ still&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\"><code>O(n²)</code></strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;after dropping constants.</span>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Step 5: Triple nested loops",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "cpp": "for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n    for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n        for (int k = 0; k < n; k++) {\n            cout << i << \" \" << j << \" \" << k << endl;\n        }\n    }\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<code>n × n × n</code>&nbsp;=&nbsp;<strong><code>O(n³)</code></strong>. Each nesting level multiplies.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "<hr style=\"border-width: 1px medium medium; border-style: solid none none; border-color: rgb(224, 224, 224) currentcolor currentcolor; border-image: none; margin: 24px 0px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Step 6: Halving the input (the log pattern)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "cpp": "int i = n;\nwhile (i > 1) {\n    cout << i << endl;\n    i = i / 2;    // i is halved each time\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Let's trace this with&nbsp;<code>n = 16</code>:&nbsp;<code>16 → 8 → 4 → 2 → 1</code>&nbsp;that's 4 steps.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<code>16 = 2⁴</code>, so the number of steps is&nbsp;<code>log₂(16) = 4</code>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Whenever you&nbsp;<strong>divide by a constant</strong>&nbsp;each iteration, the complexity is&nbsp;<strong><code>O(log n)</code></strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This is why binary search is&nbsp;<code>O(log n)</code>&nbsp;every step throws away half the remaining data.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Step 7: Combining complexities (add vs multiply)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>ADD when loops are sequential:</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "cpp": "for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {    // O(n)\n    doSomething();\n}\n\nfor (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {    // O(n)\n    doSomethingElse();\n}\n\n// Total: O(n) + O(n) = O(n)"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "MULTIPLY when loops are nested:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "cpp": "for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {           // O(n)\n    for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {       // O(n) inside\n        doSomething();\n    }\n}\n\n// Total: O(n) x O(n) = O(n^2)"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "Memory trick:&nbsp;<strong>side by side = add, inside each other = multiply</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Step 8: Recursion",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Recursion is trickier. You need to ask:&nbsp;<em>how many times does this function call itself, and with what input size?</em>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Example 1: Linear recursion:</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "cpp": "void countdown(int n) {\n    if (n == 0) return;\n    countdown(n - 1);\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Each call reduces&nbsp;<code>n</code>&nbsp;by 1. It calls itself&nbsp;<code>n</code>&nbsp;times before hitting the base case.&nbsp;<strong><code>O(n)</code></strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Example 2: The classic bad Fibonacci:</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "cpp": "int fib(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) return n;\n    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Each call spawns&nbsp;<strong>2 more calls</strong>. Draw the call tree:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "https://MyDSA-assets-images.s3.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/dsa-topics/1781208914565-680515217.png",
          "caption": ""
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The tree has roughly&nbsp;<code>2ⁿ</code>&nbsp;nodes.&nbsp;<strong><code>O(2ⁿ)</code></strong>&nbsp;catastrophically slow for large&nbsp;<code>n</code>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Example 3: Binary recursion (like merge sort):</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "cpp": "vector<int> mergeSort(vector<int> arr) {\n    if (arr.size() <= 1) return arr;\n    int mid = arr.size() / 2;\n    vector<int> left  = mergeSort({arr.begin(), arr.begin() + mid});   // half the input\n    vector<int> right = mergeSort({arr.begin() + mid, arr.end()});     // half the input\n    return merge(left, right);     // O(n) work\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "At each level, you do&nbsp;<code>O(n)</code>&nbsp;work (the merge step), and you have&nbsp;<code>log n</code>&nbsp;levels (because you halve each time). Total:&nbsp;<strong><code>O(n log n)</code></strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "4-a-visual-map-of-growth",
      "title": "4. A Visual Map of Growth",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Let's make this concrete. Here's how many operations each complexity requires for different values of&nbsp;<code>n</code>:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"https://MyDSA-assets-images.s3.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/dsa-content-images/1781423521198-19341001.png\" alt=\"ChatGPT Image Jun 11, 2026 at 11_34_36 PM.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">This is why&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">O(n²)</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;solutions pass on small inputs in interviews but time out on large ones. The shape of growth is everything.</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "Real-World DSA Examples Analyzed",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Let's walk through common patterns you'll see in every DSA course.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading4",
          "text": "1. Linear Search&nbsp;<code>O(n)</code>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "cpp": "bool linearSearch(vector<int>& arr, int target) {\n    for (int item : arr) {          // n iterations worst case\n        if (item == target) return true;\n    }\n    return false;\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Worst case: the target is at the end or not there at all. You check every element.&nbsp;<strong><code>O(n)</code></strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading4",
          "text": "2. Binary Search:&nbsp;<code>O(log n)</code>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "cpp": "def binary_search(arr, target):\n    low, high = 0, len(arr) - 1\n\n    while low <= high:\n        mid = (low + high) // 2\n        if arr[mid] == target:\n            return mid\n        elif arr[mid] < target:\n            low = mid + 1        # throw away left half\n        else:\n            high = mid - 1       # throw away right half\n\n    return -1"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Each iteration cuts the search space in half. Starting with&nbsp;<code>n</code>&nbsp;elements: after 1 step →&nbsp;<code>n/2</code>, after 2 →&nbsp;<code>n/4</code>, after&nbsp;<code>k</code>&nbsp;steps →&nbsp;<code>n/2ᵏ</code>. When does it stop? When&nbsp;<code>n/2ᵏ = 1</code>, so&nbsp;<code>k = log₂(n)</code>.&nbsp;<strong><code>O(log n)</code></strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading4",
          "text": "3. Bubble Sort:&nbsp;<code>O(n²)</code>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "cpp": "void bubbleSort(vector<int>& arr) {\n    int n = arr.size();\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {               // outer: n times\n        for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {  // inner: up to n times\n            if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1])\n                swap(arr[j], arr[j + 1]);\n        }\n    }\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Two nested loops over the array. Even though the inner loop shrinks slightly, it's still&nbsp;<code>n(n-1)/2</code>&nbsp;comparisons =&nbsp;<strong><code>O(n²)</code></strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading4",
          "text": "4. Checking for Duplicates:&nbsp;<code>O(n²)</code>&nbsp;vs&nbsp;<code>O(n)</code>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Naive approach:</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "cpp": "bool hasDuplicateSlow(vector<int>& arr) {\n    for (int i = 0; i < (int)arr.size(); i++) {\n        for (int j = i + 1; j < (int)arr.size(); j++) {   // nested loop\n            if (arr[i] == arr[j]) return true;\n        }\n    }\n    return false;\n}\n// O(n^2)"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Smart approach using a set:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "cpp": "bool hasDuplicateFast(vector<int>& arr) {\n    unordered_set<int> seen;\n    for (int item : arr) {        // single loop\n        if (seen.count(item)) return true;\n        seen.insert(item);\n    }\n    return false;\n}\n// O(n)"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Same problem, different complexities. The second is better because set lookups are&nbsp;<code>O(1)</code>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading4",
          "text": "5. Counting occurrences with a hash map:&nbsp;<code>O(n)</code>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "cpp": "unordered_map<int,int> countElements(vector<int>& arr) {\n    unordered_map<int,int> counts;\n    for (int item : arr) {           // O(n)\n        counts[item]++;              // O(1) hash lookup\n    }\n    return counts;\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "One loop, constant work per iteration.&nbsp;<strong><code>O(n)</code></strong>. Hash maps are your best friend for reducing&nbsp;<code>O(n²)</code>&nbsp;to&nbsp;<code>O(n)</code>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "5-the-mental-model-5-questions-to-ask-any-code",
      "title": "5. The Mental Model: 5 Questions to Ask Any Code",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "When you see code in an interview or while studying, ask these five questions in order:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "<strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Is there a loop?</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;If no →&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">O(1)</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">. If yes → continue.</span><br>",
            "<strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">How many times does the loop run?</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">n</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;times →&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">O(n)</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">.&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">log n</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;times (halving) →&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">O(log n)</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">.</span><br>",
            "<strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Are loops nested or sequential?</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;Sequential → add. Nested → multiply.</span><br>",
            "<strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Is there recursion?</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;Draw the call tree. Count the nodes (or branches per level × levels).</span><br>",
            "<strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">What's the dominant term?</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;Drop constants and lower-order terms. Keep the biggest one.</span><br>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "6-common-mistakes-beginners-make",
      "title": "6. Common Mistakes Beginners Make",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "<strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Mistake 1: Counting lines, not operations.</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;It doesn't matter if your loop body has 1 line or 20 lines. If there's no loop inside the loop body, it's still&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">O(n)</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">.</span><br>",
            "<strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Mistake 2: Forgetting built-in operations have complexity.</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">sorted(arr)</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;in Python is&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">O(n log n)</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">, not&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">O(1)</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">.&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">list.index(x)</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;is&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">O(n)</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">. Know your language's standard library.</span><br>",
            "<strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Mistake 3: Assuming recursion is always slow.</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;Recursion can be&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">O(log n)</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;(binary search),&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">O(n)</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;(tree traversal), or&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">O(2ⁿ)</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">(naive Fibonacci). It depends entirely on the structure of the calls.</span><br>",
            "<strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Mistake 4: Ignoring space complexity.</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;Time isn't the only resource. Creating a new array of size&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">n</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;costs&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">O(n)</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;space. Interviewers often ask you to solve problems in&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">O(1)</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;extra space.</span><br>",
            "<strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Mistake 5: Only analyzing average case.</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;Always analyze worst case unless the problem explicitly asks for average or best.</span><br>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
